   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to providing vocational\ntraining to inmates on the installation of solar hot water systems for the\nprovision of hot water to correctional facilities",
    "basePrintNo": "S3526",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3526-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 20,
       "fullName": "Zellnor Myrie",
       "imgName": "1048_Zellnor_Myrie.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1228,
       "sessionMemberId": 1825,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "MYRIE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§137 & 171, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3526\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to providing vocational\ntraining to inmates on the installation of solar hot water systems for\nthe provision of hot water to correctional facilities\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide practical and vocational training to inmates and inexpensive,\nenvironmentally sustainable hot water in prison living units in compli-\nance with the American Correctional Association standards.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection I of the bill amends section 137 of the correction law to\nprovide that, where it is practical, inmate cells, dorms and rooms\nshould be plumbed to provide hot water to wash basins.\n\nSection II of the bill amends section 171 of the correction law to state\nthat, where it is practical, inmate vocational training programs shall\nbe established to provide solar-powered hot water in facilities lacking\nrunning hot water in cells, dorms and rooms where inmates are housed.\n\nSection III sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen a convicted person is released from prison, he or she should be\nable to obtain employment and lead a law-abiding, productive life.  This\nbill would encourage the Department of Corrections to provide a voca-\ntional program in which inmates would learn to install solar-powered hot\nwater systems. Solar-powered hot water has been successfully provided at\nWallkill Correctional Facility and in other correctional facilities\nthroughout the country.\n\nMost of the 22,000 prisoners who live in prison cells do not have\nrunning hot water in their sinks, nor do some of the remaining 23,000\ninmates who live in dorms and rooms. Newer prisons have hot water\nplumbed to where inmates are housed, but most of the older prisons rely\non inmate labor to manually distribute buckets of hot water to inmates\nin their cells. Attica, Auburn, Sing Sing, Clinton, and other older\nfacilities house thousands of inmates in cold water cells. This bill\nwould provide vocational training that could result in future employ-\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
