    "lawCode": "Amd §§137 & 171, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3526A\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to providing vocational\ntraining to incarcerated individuals on the installation of solar hot\nwater systems for the provision of hot water to correctional facilities\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo provide practical and vocational training to incarcerated individuals\nand inexpensive, environmentally sustainable hot water in prison living\nunits in compliance with the American Correctional Association stand-\nards.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection I of the bill amends section 137 of the correction law to\nprovide that, where it is practical, cells, dorms and rooms should be\nplumbed to provide hot water to wash basins.\n\nSection II of the bill amends section 171 of the correction law to state\nthat, where it is practical, vocational training programs for incarcer-\nated individuals shall be established to provide solar-powered hot water\nin facilities lacking running hot water in cells, dorms and rooms where\nincarcerated individuals are housed.\n\nSection III sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen a convicted person is released from prison, he or she should be\nable to obtain employment and lead a law-abiding, productive life.  This\nbill would encourage the Department of Corrections to provide a voca-\ntional program in which incarcerated individuals would learn to install\nsolar-powered hot water systems. Solar-powered hot water has been\nsuccessfully provided at Wallkill Correctional Facility and in other\ncorrectional facilities throughout the country.\n\nMost of the 22,000 prisoners who live in prison cells do not have\nrunning hot water in their sinks, nor do some of the remaining 23,000\nincarcerated individuals who live in dorms and rooms. Newer prisons have\nhot water plumbed to where incarcerated individuals are housed, but most\nof the older prisons rely on the labor of incarcerated individuals to\nmanually distribute buckets of hot water to incarcerated individuals in\ntheir cells.\n\nAttica, Auburn, Sing Sing, Clinton, and other older facilities house\nthousands of incarcerated individuals in cold water cells. This bill\nwould provide vocational training that could result in future employ-\nment. The use of renewable energy sources would not only reduce green-\nhouse emissions, but also save the state money by lowering the energy\ncosts of facilities.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S.4309 - Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and Correction\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON STATE:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect six months after it shall have become law.\n",
